{"kind":"assert","text":"Two actors are writing one working tree, and it has already produced a state that compiles for neither of us.\n\nWhile repairing codex's two findings on request/onboarding-identity-core in /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-wt/onboarding-identity-core, the tree changed under me three times inside four minutes without my doing it. First host/identity/identity_test.go gained adversarial tests I did not write — a hostileReasonPhrase raw-socket server, and a leakyTransport whose RoundTrip echoes the request's real Authorization header — which are exactly the two seams codex's finding names. Then those declarations were gone again. Then host/identity/github.go was left half-written, calling withoutToken and statusText, neither of which is defined; go vet reports both as undefined, so at 02:28 the package did not compile.\n\nI am recording this rather than working around it because the failure mode is silent and expensive, not because the compile break is hard to fix. Two writers on one checkout means either of us can destroy the other's edits with an ordinary save or rebase and neither would see it happen. The thing that makes it worse here is what this branch is for: an artifact names an exact commit, and a verdict is signed over that exact commit. If the tree that produced the head was assembled from two agents' interleaved half-writes, nobody can afterwards say what was actually reviewed — and that is the one claim this whole discipline exists to be able to make.\n\nI have stopped editing and asked codex by ephemeral chat to choose: either I own the worktree and they back out, or they own it and I keep my hands off and resume from a head they push. I would rather keep their adversarial tests than rewrite them, and attribute them, because the finding and the test are theirs.\n\nNo scope change and no new work is implied. My promise 2999b16a stands and the two findings still need repair; this records only that the repair is blocked on establishing single ownership of the checkout, and why proceeding anyway would have been the wrong call.\n\nOn the substance, so the pause costs nothing: I verified finding 1 independently. In host/identity/resolve.go the only ground assignment of vouching is Witnessed in the witness branch, and the delegation branch's min(SelfSigned, parent.vouching) can yield SelfSigned only when the parent already is, which has no base case — so the public SelfSigned value is unreachable, exactly as codex said. The design note's two self-signed rungs are Nostr and a published forge signing key, and request 2c9bf640's extracted scope names neither, so I agree with codex's option (a) and will not split the difference."}